// pkg/mysql/decimal.test.bit — the DECIMAL/NUMERIC codec proven against a real
// server's own text form (#3995), plus the non-live error paths.
//
// THE CORPUS IS THE POINT. `decimalCorpus()` is 200 checked-in values covering
// digit-group boundaries (MySQL's binary DECIMAL packs digits in base-1e9
// groups), leading and trailing zero groups, negative and negative-zero, and
// the true max/min this driver's `decimal` can hold. Every value is inserted
// into ONE real column and read back TWO independent ways from the same row —
// the plain binary value (`mysqlDecimal`, this codec) and
// `cast(amount as char)` parsed by `parseDecimal` (std/decimal, #3983,
// nothing this package wrote) — so the oracle never shares code with the
// thing it is checking. A codec proven only against values it produced itself
// proves nothing.
//
// The column is `decimal(29,9)`: MySQL fixes a column's SCALE, so every
// stored value is padded or rounded to exactly 9 fractional digits regardless
// of how many the inserted text had — which is also how the corpus stays
// inside Bit's `decimal` (mantissa <= 2^96-1, 29 significant digits, scale
// <= 28): 20 integer digits plus a 9-digit scale is 29, the type's own
// ceiling. `decimalTrailingZeroTest` and `decimalOverflowTest` below use
// their own columns because they are testing the two things a shared column
// cannot: a SMALLER scale where "1.50" must not collapse to "1.5", and a
// value that must NOT fit.

// The 200-value corpus, split into three parts to stay under this file's own
// function-length lint ceiling (80 lines) -- combined by decimalCorpus below.
// See this file's header for what the values cover.
